---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Open questions"
      eyebrow="Published so strangers can help"
      dek="Published deliberately. An archive that lists only its answers is advertising; one that lists its questions can be helped by strangers.">
<section class="blk">
<h2>1. The name of the great-grandfather</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The blocking question.</strong> Nothing else can proceed far without it — not the consular
    lists, not the stud books, not the Argentine registers.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Read David Defranceski's <strong>MyHeritage tree</strong> for the Lerena branch, with its stored
      facts, citations and media.</li>
    <li>Obtain <strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena's birth record</strong>, which names her father.</li>
    <li>Sweep <strong>NAAIRS</strong> for every Lerena estate file in South Africa. With 61 bearers in the
      country this is a small, finite search, and estate files name the whole household.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>2. Was he really a Consul General?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Search the <strong>Government Gazette</strong> for the <em>exequatur</em> — the host state's formal
      recognition of a foreign consul. Crisp, dated, decisive.</li>
    <li>Search the Argentine <strong>Boletín Oficial</strong> and the Foreign Ministry's diplomatic lists
      for the appointment.</li>
    <li><strong>Test the honorary-consul hypothesis.</strong> A resident merchant holding an honorary post
      would appear in the gazettes but not in the career foreign-service lists — and would reconcile a
      diplomat with a man who bred horses and farmed ostriches.
      <span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span></li>
    <li>Establish <strong>which post</strong>: Cape Town, Johannesburg, Pretoria or Durban, and in which
      years.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>3. The horses</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Search the <strong>South African Stud Book</strong> and Jockey Club registers for Lerena as owner,
      breeder or importer — particularly <strong>imports from Argentina</strong>, which would corroborate
      the claim directly.</li>
    <li>Search the <strong>Argentine Stud Book</strong> at the other end.</li>
    <li>Establish who <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong> was in family terms — his dates,
      household and descendants — and whether he connects to this line at all.
      <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a></li>
    <li>Trace <strong>Haras El Viejo</strong>: its records, its sale or dispersal, and its succession.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>4. The ostrich farm</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Search the <strong>Oudtshoorn</strong> and Little Karoo farm records, and the C.P. Nel Museum's
      holdings on the feather trade.</li>
    <li>Test the date. The feather boom collapsed in <strong>1914</strong>; if the farm was real it most
      likely predates that, which in turn constrains when the family arrived.</li>
    <li>Search the deeds registry for land held under the surname.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>5. Basque or Extremaduran?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Obtain the <strong>INE provincial distribution</strong> for Spain. Forebears summarises the Spanish
      Lerenas as chiefly <strong>La Rioja, Andalusia and Madrid</strong> — awkward for both theories, and it
      needs the real figures rather than a summary.</li>
    <li>Look for <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Llerena</em> <strong>alternating for the same individual</strong> in
      River Plate records. That, more than any etymology, settles whether the two names are one.</li>
    <li>Find the emigration record, which normally names the town of origin outright.
      <a href={u("/name")}>The name &rarr;</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>6. Rosario, and the crossing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Search <strong>Santa Fe</strong> civil registration and the Rosario parish registers for the family.</li>
    <li>Search <strong>CEMLA</strong> and the Buenos Aires passenger lists for the arrival from Europe.</li>
    <li>Find the <strong>outbound crossing to South Africa</strong> — the date, the ship, and who travelled.</li>
    <li>Keep <strong>Uruguay</strong> live throughout. It has the world's densest Lerena population, and if
      Argentina stalls it is the next move, not the last resort.
      <a href={u("/argentina")}>Argentina &rarr;</a></li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>7. The South African kindred</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Establish whether the <strong>61 South African Lerenas</strong> are indeed one family, and whether
      this line founded it. <a href={u("/south-africa")}>South Africa &rarr;</a></li>
    <li>Work out the <strong>Booyzen</strong> marriage — an Afrikaner connection, and evidence of settlement
      rather than a posting.</li>
    <li>Test whether <strong>Taylor</strong> and <strong>Barry</strong> attach to this line or to another
      branch of the tree. <span class="chip inf">Unexamined</span></li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Recorded so they are not repeated</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>Chrome extension was not connected</strong> on 8 September 2026, so the MyHeritage tree
      could not be read in this session. It requires a logged-in browser session; it is not a public source.</li>
    <li><strong>Forebears' Spain page</strong> for the surname returned no content to an automated fetch. The
      provincial breakdown will need another route — the INE, or a read by hand.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
</Base>
